Today I spent some time learning about **Kubernetes Ingress** and understanding why it is important when running applications in a production environment.

• Learned the problem of exposing multiple Kubernetes Services externally using separate **LoadBalancer Services**, which can become costly and difficult to manage as the number of applications grows.

• Understood how **Ingress provides a centralized entry point** for HTTP/HTTPS traffic and allows multiple applications to be accessed through a common external endpoint.

• Learned how Ingress can intelligently route incoming requests to the correct Kubernetes Service based on **hostnames and URL paths**.

• Understood **path-based routing**, for example:
`/` → Frontend Service
`/api` → Backend Service
`/admin` → Admin Service

• Also learned **host-based routing**, where different domains or subdomains can route traffic to different Services.

• Clearly understood the difference between an **Ingress Resource** and an **Ingress Controller** — the Ingress defines the routing rules, while the Ingress Controller actually implements those rules and handles the incoming traffic.

• Learned that Ingress does not directly replace Kubernetes Services. The typical flow is:
**User → External LoadBalancer/Entry Point → Ingress Controller → Service → Pod**

• Explored how Ingress can also handle **HTTPS/TLS**, making it possible to manage secure application access through a centralized routing layer.

• Most importantly, I understood why Ingress is useful for building a more **scalable, organized, and manageable architecture** when multiple applications need to be exposed from a Kubernetes cluster.

Continuing to build my Kubernetes knowledge step by step and getting closer to understanding how production-grade Kubernetes environments are designed. 🚀☸️

